+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
+    """
+    Reassemble the three scattered mirror-tiles into the framed four-quadrant mosaic and repair every magenta erasure.
+
+    Input structure:
+    - A grey 1-cell frame encloses a 2h×2w target area whose top-left h×w quadrant contains a colored tile fragment with magenta erased cells, while the other three quadrants are black.
+    - Outside the frame lie exactly three separate h×w rectangular tiles, also using magenta as an erasure color.
+    - Ignoring magenta cells, the outside tiles are the left-right mirror, the up-down mirror, and the 180-degree rotation of the true top-left pattern.
+
+    Rule:
+    - Infer the framed region size from the grey border and read the top-left quadrant fragment.
+    - Extract the three outside h×w tiles and test which one must be the horizontal mirror, vertical mirror, or 180-degree rotation by undoing each transform and checking pixelwise agreement on every non-erased observation.
+    - Reconstruct the unique underlying h×w pattern from the four partial observations, then output the repaired 2h×2w mirror mosaic with quadrants [original, left-right mirror; up-down mirror, 180-degree rotation].
+
+    Key insight:
+    - Each outside tile becomes comparable only after reversing its candidate symmetry transform; the correct assignment is the unique one whose four partial views agree cellwise and cover every orbit at least once.
